n one of  the people at the event  said I never voted for an n IG ger  before but I'm gonna vote for you since  last year Green has held over a dozen  screenings in Buffalo we are one of the  most segregated cities in America  followed by a diverse panel of community  leaders who discuss the film no topic is  off the table  there's no black people in my church  where are they why don't they come I'm  wondering what do you think the  challenges are specifically for young  women and girls of color when you watch  the white is project and you watch the  black this project you can see this very  clearly and that the whiteness project  is clearly very unsophisticated do you  feel like people are ever too quick to  call out behavior as oh you're just  being racist absolutely everything is  not race related so you know if you  didn't do something right and you know  someone corrects it doesn't mean it's  the color of your skin the screening  show how talking honestly about race can  be liberating Green says it's  therapeutic for him and his audiences  all our screen has been predominately  white is that encouraging it's I can't  even tell you what it does for me it it  gets me emotional because here are  people maybe they don't understand my  story or any other story but the fact  that they want to understand says so  much we're going in the right direction  filmmaker Cory green and producer Pete  Johnson plan to screen this film again  this spring and you know they really  said the most impactful moments for them  were people walking away with an  understanding that they didn't have  before and I think that's what makes it  different - we can talk about having a  conversation but they were very  intentional about how they put this  documentary together and then they're  having these screenings where they're  opening it opening it up for dialogue  what's so interesting that that the  audiences are predominantly white that  is that says something right there I'm  curious qu